2. In your project, add the JavaFX libraries to the classpath. You can do this by adding the following line to your build.gradle file:

    ```
    implementation 'org.openjfx:javafx-controls:14'
    ```

3. Create a new JavaFX application. To do this, create a new class that extends the javafx.application.Application class. Override the start() method to define the primary stage and any user interface elements that you want to display.

   ```
   public class MyJavaFXApp extends Application {
       public void start(Stage primaryStage) throws Exception {
           // Add code to define the user interface elements here
       }
   }
   ```

4. In your VueAdministraton class, create an instance of the MyJavaFXApp class and launch it. You can do this by calling the launch() method of the Application class.

   ```
   public void launchJavaFX() {
      MyJavaFXApp app = new MyJavaFXApp();
      app.launch();
   }
   ```

5. Finally, call the launchJavaFX() method from your main() method to start the JavaFX application.

   ```
   public static void main(String[] args) {
      VueAdministraton vue = new VueAdministraton();
      vue.launchJavaFX();
   }
   ```

With these steps, you should now be able to run a JavaFX application within your VueAdministraton class. You can add more user interface elements and events to interact with your application as needed.

