The `java.lang.IllegalStateException: Toolkit not initialized` error indicates that you are trying to use JavaFX before it has been properly initialized. This typically occurs when you try to create a JavaFX component (such as a Scene or Stage) before calling the `launch()` method of the `Application` class.

Here are some common causes of this error and how to fix them:

1. Not extending the `Application` class: If you are creating a JavaFX application, make sure that your main class extends the `Application` class and overrides the `start()` method. This allows the JavaFX runtime to properly initialize the toolkit. Here is an example of how to properly structure your `Main` class:

```java
import javafx.application.Application;
import javafx.stage.Stage;

public class Main extends Application {
    @Override
    public void start(Stage primaryStage) {
        // Create and display your JavaFX user interface here
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        launch(args); // This must be called to initialize the toolkit
    }
}
```

2. Calling JavaFX components too early: If you are creating a JavaFX component (such as a Scene or Stage) before calling the `launch()` method, you may get the `Toolkit not initialized` error. Make sure that you only create JavaFX components inside the `start()` method of your `Application` class.

3. Using JavaFX classes on a different thread: If you are using JavaFX classes (such as `Stage` or `Scene`) on a thread other than the JavaFX application thread, you may get the `Toolkit not initialized` error. Make sure that you only access JavaFX components from the JavaFX application thread, using techniques such as `Platform.runLater()`.

I hope this helps you resolve the issue.

